---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="Finance"
    categorySlug="guides"
    subcategorySlug="finance"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">I Lost $2,300 Moving My Own Money</h2>
  <p>
    First year as a nomad. American client pays me in USD. I'm living in Lisbon, spending euros. Every month I'd
    transfer money through my US bank and watch chunks of it disappear. Thought I was just paying normal fees.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then I actually ran the numbers. That $2,300? All exchange rate markup. My bank was quietly scraping 3% off every
    transfer while advertising "competitive rates."
  </p>
  <p>
    Turns out there's a whole world of multi-currency accounts built for people like us. Fifty-plus currencies in one
    place, local bank details in a bunch of countries, conversion fees under 0.5%. Would've been nice to know about
    this before I donated twenty-three hundred bucks to Chase.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Traditional Banks Fail -->
  <h2 id="traditional-banks">Why Your Regular Bank Keeps Draining You</h2>

  <h3>The Exchange Rate Scam Nobody Mentions</h3>
  <p>
    Banks love advertising "free" international transfers. What they don't mention is how badly they're marking up the
    exchange rate. We're talking 2-4% worse than what currencies are actually trading at.
  </p>
  <p>
    Move five grand somewhere? That's a hundred to two hundred bucks vanishing into thin air. Do that once a month and
    you're basically handing your bank fifteen hundred to twenty-four hundred a year for the privilege of moving your
    own money.
  </p>

  <h3>The Geographical Nonsense</h3>
  <p>
    So many banks just can't handle the nomad lifestyle:
  </p>
  <p>
    Logging in from a Thai IP address? Frozen. Need to change something basic on your account? Come into a branch, they
    say . from the other side of the planet. Stay abroad too long and some institutions just close your account
    entirely. And forget about using an international phone number for verification.
  </p>

  <h3>The Currency Limitations</h3>
  <p>
    Most traditional banks might . if you're lucky . offer accounts in three to five currencies. Everything gets
    funneled through your home currency, which means bleeding money on every single transaction.
  </p>

  <h3>Transfers That Make You Wait and Pay</h3>
  <p>
    International wires through regular banks come with their own special torture:
  </p>
  <p>
    Twenty-five to fifty bucks per transfer. Three to five business days of wondering where your money is. Correspondent
    banks along the way each grabbing their slice. Money showing up with chunks missing and nobody quite able to explain
    why.
  </p>

  <!-- Best Multi-Currency Accounts -->
  <h2 id="best-accounts">The Services Actually Worth Using</h2>

  <h3>Wise . My Go-To for Client Payments</h3>
  <p>
    What it does best: Receiving money from clients, keeping fees transparent, and supporting basically every currency
    that exists.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hold over fifty currencies and get local banking details in ten-plus countries. Their rates stick close to what
    currencies actually trade at, with a small fee tacked on . usually around half a percent. You'll get proper account
    numbers for the US, UK, Europe, Australia, New Zealand, and more. Free card included.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is the one I point <a href="/tools/best-invoicing-tools">freelancers</a> toward when they're getting paid from
    different places.
  </p>

  <h3>Revolut . For the Day-to-Day</h3>
  <p>
    What it does best: Everyday purchases, slick app experience, and airport currency swaps.
  </p>
  <p>
    Thirty-plus currencies in the app. Real interbank rates during business hours, though they add a little markup on
    weekends when markets are closed. Free tier handles basic needs; paid tiers unlock more perks. Built-in budgeting,
    crypto trading if you're into that, virtual cards for sketchy websites.
  </p>
  <p>
    I use this for coffee and groceries and random purchases while <a href="/blog/top-destinations-digital-nomads">traveling</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>N26 . The European Option</h3>
  <p>
    What it does best: Smooth experience for folks based in Europe.
  </p>
  <p>
    It's euro-centric rather than truly multi-currency. Zero fees across the eurozone, though costs pop up elsewhere. EU
    banking license means deposits are protected up to a hundred grand. If you're spending most of your time in Europe,
    this works great.
  </p>
  <p>
    Heads up though . not available if you're US-based, and the multi-currency features don't stack up against Wise or
    Revolut.
  </p>

  <h3>Payoneer . Platform Payments</h3>
  <p>
    What it does best: Collecting earnings from marketplaces and freelance platforms.
  </p>
  <p>
    Receive in seven major currencies and get paid directly from Amazon, Fiverr, Upwork, and a bunch of other platforms
    without extra steps. Withdrawal options reach a hundred fifty countries.
  </p>
  <p>
    The withdrawal fees run higher than Wise, but if your <a href="/tools/accept-international-payments-freelancer">income
    flows through integrated platforms</a>, the convenience
    sometimes makes it worth the hit.
  </p>

  <!-- Feature Comparison -->
  <h2 id="comparison">Feature Comparison Table</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto my-6">
    <table class="w-full text-sm">
      <thead>
      <tr class="border-b border-white/20">
        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-white font-semibold">Feature</th>
        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-white font-semibold">Wise</th>
        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-white font-semibold">Revolut</th>
        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-white font-semibold">N26</th>
        <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-white font-semibold">Payoneer</th>
      </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody class="text-white/80">
      <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
        <td class="py-3 px-4 font-medium">Currencies held</td>
