        <td class="py-3 px-4">Platforms</td>
      </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-white/60">*Revolut charges 0.5-1% markup on weekends and above monthly limits</p>

  <!-- Fees & Exchange Rates -->
  <h2 id="fees">What the Rates and Fees Actually Mean</h2>

  <h3>The Rate Banks Don't Want You Knowing</h3>
  <p>
    That "mid-market rate" or "interbank rate" everyone references? It's the actual exchange rate . the midpoint between
    buying and selling prices that big institutions use when trading among themselves. Search any currency pair on
    Google and that's what shows up.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your bank isn't giving you that rate. They're padding it by 2-4% and hoping you don't notice. A transfer advertised
    as "fee-free" can still cost you three percent once you look at what exchange rate they used.
  </p>

  <h3>How Wise Does the Math</h3>
  <p>
    Wise shows the actual mid-market rate, then adds a clear percentage on top:
  </p>
  <p>
    Converting dollars to euros runs around 0.41%. Pounds to euros typically hits 0.35%. Dollars to pounds lands
    somewhere near 0.46%.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything gets spelled out before you hit confirm. No guessing games.
  </p>

  <h3>The Revolut Approach</h3>
  <p>
    Their model has some conditions attached:
  </p>
  <p>
    Weekdays get you the raw interbank rate up to your plan's monthly limit. Weekends add half a percent to one percent
    because markets are technically closed. Go over your limit and similar markup kicks in.
  </p>

  <h3>Putting Real Numbers On It</h3>
  <p>
    That same five thousand dollar conversion:
  </p>
  <p>
    Through a traditional bank? Roughly a hundred fifty to two hundred disappears. Through Wise? About twenty-five
    bucks. Through Revolut on a weekday under your limit? Basically nothing.
  </p>

  <!-- Debit Cards -->
  <h2 id="debit-cards">Cards and How to Use Them</h2>

  <h3>The Wise Card</h3>
  <p>
    Swipe anywhere Mastercard works and pay in whatever currency at near-real exchange rates plus their small fee. Pull
    cash from ATMs free up to around two hundred pounds monthly, then pay 1.75% after that. Lock and unlock the thing
    instantly through the app if something feels off.
  </p>

  <h3>The Revolut Card</h3>
  <p>
    Spending happens at interbank rates when you're within plan limits and not on a weekend. ATM withdrawal limits
    depend on your subscription level. Virtual cards let you keep your real number hidden for online shopping.
    Disposable virtual cards take security even further.
  </p>

  <h3>The Payoneer Card</h3>
  <p>
    This one costs about thirty bucks yearly. Each ATM withdrawal runs just over three dollars. Best reserved for
    pulling your Payoneer balance when you need physical cash.
  </p>

  <h3>Smart Habits With Cards</h3>
  <p>
    Always pick local currency when the terminal asks. Machines love offering to convert for you . decline every time.
    Some cards want travel notifications; set those up before you land somewhere unexpected. Keep at least two cards
    from different providers because one will inevitably stop working at the worst moment. When you need cash, fewer big
    withdrawals beat lots of small ones.
  </p>

  <!-- Country Availability -->
  <h2 id="availability">Where Each Service Actually Works</h2>

  <p>
    Wise covers residents across a hundred sixty countries. The US, UK, Europe, Australia, and most of Asia and Africa
    can sign up, though specific features shift depending on location.
  </p>

  <p>
    Revolut dominates in Britain and Europe. Americans get access to basic accounts only. Lots of Asian and African
    countries are just blocked entirely.
  </p>

  <p>
    N26 sticks to the EU and surrounding economic area. They actually left the US market back in 2022. Not really a
    global solution.
  </p>

  <p>
    Payoneer operates in over two hundred countries and stays strong in places where other services haven't bothered
    expanding . India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, and similar markets. Solid backup option if your country doesn't support
    the others.
  </p>

  <!-- Setting Up Your System -->
  <h2 id="system-setup">Building Your Setup</h2>

  <h3>The Stack Most Nomads Land On</h3>
  <p>
    Having multiple accounts covers different needs:
  </p>
  <p>
    Keep a bank back home for <a href="/guides/freelancer-tax-deductions-guide">taxes</a>, maintaining credit history,
    and emergencies. Set up Wise as the main spot for
    client payments in various currencies. Add Revolut or N26 for everyday spending and travel purchases. Throw in
    Payoneer if any of your income flows through platforms that integrate with it.
  </p>

  <h3>Getting Paid From Anywhere</h3>
  <p>
    Grab local banking details in whatever currencies you regularly receive:
  </p>
  <p>
    American clients get your US account info and send cheap ACH transfers. British clients use your UK sort code and
    account number. European clients send to your SEPA-compatible IBAN.
  </p>
  <p>
    Clients pay using their normal local transfer methods . fast and free on their end . and the money lands in that
    currency ready for you.
  </p>

  <h3>Converting at the Right Moment</h3>
  <p>
    Don't let anything auto-convert. Instead:
  </p>
  <p>
    Stockpile balances in currencies you know you'll spend soon. Watch for favorable rates . Wise shows charts of how
    currencies have moved. Batch your conversions rather than switching small amounts constantly. Stay away from weekend
    conversions on Revolut when markups apply.
  </p>

  <h3>Spending Without Thinking Too Hard</h3>
  <p>
    Use whichever card connects to your balance in the local currency. Sitting in Berlin with euros in your account?
    Spend from the euro balance. Don't have local currency loaded? The card handles conversion automatically at decent
    rates. Let the app sort <a href="/tools/best-expense-tracking-apps">expenses into categories</a> so you can see
    where money actually goes.
  </p>

  <!-- FAQ Section -->
  <FAQSection faqs={faqs}/>

  <h2>Stop Letting Banks Take Your Cut</h2>
  <p>
    Regular banking wasn't designed for people earning in one currency while spending in another and crossing borders
    constantly. These multi-currency tools were built exactly for that.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What to actually do:</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Open a Wise account for receiving payments . takes maybe ten minutes. Order their free card. Set up local banking
    details wherever you receive money. Consider adding Revolut if you spend lots of time in Europe. Don't close your
    home country account since you'll need it for backup and local obligations.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quit handing over thousands each year to sneaky bank fees. Your money should work as globally as your lifestyle
    does.
  </p>

  <!-- Related Articles -->
  <Fragment slot="full-width">
    <RelatedArticles articles={relatedArticles}/>
  </Fragment>
</ArticleLayout>
